<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="fff349a0-c2b3-4147-8048-0595272170a0">
  <data key="d3">Advances under Rider 3 are paid without any reduction from actuarial discounting. This differs from Rider 2, where each year’s payout is diminished by about 8%.</data>
  <data key="d4">differs from</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="586acb2a-853b-4ebd-8a84-7e996761219c">
  <data key="d3">Since no actuarial discount is applied to advances from the Chronic Illness Accelerated Death Benefit rider, recipients receive the full benefit amount regardless of life expectancy assumptions, even though the rider carries a $48 yearly premium.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="25532b26-15cf-439e-81bb-d1a42ef63fe9">
  <data key="d3">Advances paid out under Rider 3 are made without an actuarial discount, ensuring full monetary value. These advances are available only when the insured meets the qualifying condition of being unable to perform two or more Activities of Daily Living due to chronic illness.</data>
  <data key="d4">payment structure</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="8939b21f-f68d-40db-89a6-72aed8a58ea7">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an actuarial discount means that advances taken under Rider 3 are not reduced by expected future costs, so they align directly with the maximum allowable monthly withdrawal of 2% of the face amount.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences indirectly</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="63574a8d-028b-4e11-96b6-a8a861f0e4a5">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an actuarial discount ensures that advances taken under Rider 3 are not reduced by future cost-of-living adjustments or mortality risks. This condition reinforces the significance of the 50% cap set in Fact Sentence 1, ensuring clarity about the actual disbursement size regardless of underlying risk factors.</data>
  <data key="d4">enhances</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9074adae-f9ba-4a37-87a1-710b818029e7" target="c37a8c56-c455-4223-bf63-099ad8e97416">
  <data key="d3">Rider 3's absence of an actuarial discount, noted in fact_sentence_2, explains part of why the combined premium reaches $1,089 annually, supporting the figure stated in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains cost</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
