  <data key="d3">Since war-related deaths are explicitly excluded, they cannot trigger the $750,000 accidental death benefit even if the cause appears accidental.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="4351e296-ad01-4b03-8073-cffdc596ce2d">
  <data key="d3">While fact_sentence_2 excludes war-related deaths from the accidental death benefit, it operates within the broader framework established by fact_sentence_1—that the accident must be the direct cause of death—thereby reinforcing the specificity required for qualification.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="b938585a-fc9e-4358-bb77-95bbcf1e1f31">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 excludes war-related deaths from qualification, reinforcing the need for any eligible claim to meet the additional timing criterion outlined in fact_sentence_1—that death occurs within 90 days of the accident.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="8ea7b659-7380-42ed-b6f1-e4cffbb45c59">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 specifies that deaths resulting from war are ineligible for the accidental death benefit. Like suicide, it serves as a distinct exclusionary condition unrelated to the cause described in sentence one.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="8f0204f1-a4f1-4add-9ba8-59760c0094b2">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 identifies war-related deaths as ineligible for the death benefit. Fact sentence 1 further clarifies that self-inflicted injuries are similarly excluded. These statements jointly expand the list of non-qualifying circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="2e9feb03-b57e-4d0b-913c-d96f91607819">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that deaths due to war are not covered, while fact sentence 2 adds that intoxication also voids eligibility. Both serve as distinct exclusion criteria preventing claim payouts under the accidental death benefit.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="aedfd2a5-0d7e-477f-952c-d53e51f6f0f2">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that deaths due to war are not covered, while fact sentence 2 adds that intoxication—specifically drug use—at the time of death also voids benefits. Both serve as exclusion clauses limiting claim eligibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="58b732d1-91fb-4e00-8dd8-6c20dca4bfb3">
  <data key="d3">The exclusion of war-related deaths reinforces the broader limitation on non-intentional but high-risk causes of death, aligning with the exclusion of aviation accidents where the insured was involved professionally. Both clauses serve to limit liability for certain types of fatal incidents deemed outside standard accident coverage.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="878ac5c5-197f-4b9e-9430-2a1fd25c7ff0">
  <data key="d3">While the accidental death benefit costs $94 yearly, it does not cover deaths due to war, meaning the premium may be paid without triggering payout under certain fatal incidents covered otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="5006f094-181a-470d-ac3b-c7b521d9de76">
  <data key="d3">While fact_sentence_2 excludes war-related deaths from the death benefit, fact_sentence_1 confirms that non-war accidental injuries still qualify for the $750,000 payout, reinforcing the boundary defined by the exclusion clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="9b19b8c2-d328-414e-b271-76cab099ce37">
  <data key="d3">The exclusion of war-related deaths undermines potential claims under Rider 5, which demands the accident be the direct cause of death, thereby limiting its scope.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c9a479ac-a21d-4893-8d96-0ee391ee4cb0" target="d85e868a-f03c-4843-904f-562570994fbd">
  <data key="d3">While the 90-day window defines when death must occur after an accident, it does not apply to war-related fatalities, which are explicitly barred regardless of timing.</data>
  <data key="d4">excludes under</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
